<h2 id="why-theyre-catching-onfast">Why They’re Catching On—Fast</h2>

<p>So, why are these things popping up everywhere—from major universities to job boards?</p>

<p><br /></p>

<h3 id="lets-look-at-it-from-a-few-angles">Let’s look at it from a few angles:</h3>

<p>For Students</p>

<ol>
  <li>
    <p>You can share your credentials online, with just a link.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>They’re perfect for LinkedIn profiles, digital portfolios, and job applications.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>They let you show off specific skills. Not just “B.A. in Sociology” but “Data Analysis,” “Research Methods,” “Public Policy Writing.”</p>
  </li>
</ol>

<p>For University Staff</p>

<ol>
  <li>
    <p>A supported digital check can reduce some manual work. Authority, recipient matching and unavailable resources may still require official enquiries.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>They help reduce fraud. You’d be surprised how many fake diplomas float around.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>They allow for micro-credentials, giving students recognition even for short programs, workshops, or co-curriculars.</p>
  </li>
</ol>

<p>For Employers</p>

<ol>
  <li>
    <p>A badge can describe an assessed spreadsheet task. Its criteria and evidence—not the image alone—support a capability claim.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Supported proof can check integrity; competence and issuer authority remain separate evaluation questions.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>They get a clearer picture of what you can actually do—not just what degree you earned.</p>
  </li>
</ol>

