<h2 id="what-is-already-standardizedand-what-remains-a-forecast">What is already standardized—and what remains a forecast</h2>

<p>“Digital credential” is a broad category that can include documents or hosted records. A <strong>verifiable credential</strong> uses structured issuer claims and an appropriate securing mechanism. The <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/vc-data-model-2.0/">W3C VC Data Model 2.0</a> is a Recommendation dated 15 May 2025; it does not independently prove learner competence or promise universal acceptance.</p>

<p>Institutional adoption, employer recognition and future wallet support remain context-dependent. Export a sample into the real receiver and agree key/status continuity rather than assuming a record will work forever. For a university, the registrar governs source records, academic teams approve achievement meaning and IT verifies the receiving interfaces. <a href="/comprehensive-learner-record">Comprehensive Learner Record governance</a> addresses connected records, while the <a href="/blog/Understanding-W3C-Verifiable-Credentials.html">W3C technical guide</a> explains proof boundaries. These are implementation questions, not a forecast of SEO or employment gains.</p>
